WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.135
You type Crazy Heart into Spotify and the matches come back before you finish typing.

00:00:05.135 --> 00:00:08.269
Behind that box sit one hundred million tracks.

00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:11.404
Read every row to check the title and one search is one hundred million reads.

00:00:11.404 --> 00:00:14.539
Do that per keystroke and the system falls over.

00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:17.673
Spotify never reads the table.

00:00:17.673 --> 00:00:20.808
It looks the words up.

00:00:20.808 --> 00:00:26.081
The structure that makes it work is an inverted index, and it works like the index at the back of a textbook.

00:00:26.081 --> 00:00:31.353
Start on the left with the term dictionary.

00:00:31.353 --> 00:00:36.626
Every unique word in the Songs table, held in a B-plus tree on disk.

00:00:36.626 --> 00:00:41.899
Look up Crazy, look up Heart, two quick tree descents.

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:47.171
And the dictionary is a B-plus tree on purpose, not a hash index.

00:00:47.171 --> 00:00:52.444
A tree keeps neighbouring words in sorted order, so Craz sits right beside Crazy and the engine can autocomplete you as you type.

00:00:52.444 --> 00:00:57.717
A hash index would scatter them into random buckets and never finish a prefix.

00:00:57.717 --> 00:01:02.990
Each word points at its postings list on the right, a packed array of every song i-d that contains that word.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:08.262
Now you have two sorted lists.

00:01:08.262 --> 00:01:13.535
Pull both into memory and intersect them in RAM, walking them in lockstep and keeping only the i-ds in both.

00:01:13.535 --> 00:01:18.808
For Crazy Heart that leaves song i-ds twelve and one-oh-eight.

00:01:18.808 --> 00:01:24.080
Two term lookups, two postings reads, one intersection, then read just those two data pages.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.353
About ten reads total, against one hundred million for a full scan.

00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:36.145
That is the whole idea.

00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:42.936
An inverted index turns a scan of one hundred million rows into a tree lookup and an intersection in memory, and the same tree that finds Crazy completes Craz as you type.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:49.728
Real search then layers ranking on top, scoring each match by where the word hit, popularity, and your history.

00:01:49.728 --> 00:01:56.520
Next the workload flips from read-heavy search to write-heavy ingestion, and the index has to flip with it.
